YAML scenario reference

This file tells scenario authors what keys exist, which values pass
lint, and what the runner proves. Read the worked example first to
see a full file, then the key sections to author, then Limits before
submitting. When this file and older prose disagree, the code wins.
Lint with:

  make lint

Run with bin/rsuite run (needs root for netns plus TUN). List with
bin/rsuite list. Integers accept bare or quoted digits. Extra keys
fail lint. If lint fails, fix the named key and rerun lint: nothing
ran yet. If you skip this reference and copy an old file, you will
fail lint on a renamed or scoped key.

Top-level keys

The top map names the system under test, the cluster size, the
backstop timeout, and the phases that prove behavior. Get the SUT
key right first: it gates every scoped key below, and a weld-only
key on a concord file (or reverse) fails lint. The top map allows
exactly 13 keys. Unknown keys fail lint.

name. Required. Non-empty string, max 63 chars. Scope: both. Wrong
shape fails lint; a blank or over-long name never runs.

  name: mesh-two

version. Required. Int equal to 1. Scope: both. Any other number
fails lint; version pins the schema.

  version: 1

sut. Required. String concord or weld. Scope: both. It gates every
SUT scoped key below. Pick wrong and half the file fails.

  sut: concord

nodes. Required. Int 1..100. Scope: both. All node indices in the
file must be 0..nodes-1. Too many nodes fails lint; an index past
the end fails lint too.

  nodes: 2

needs. Optional. List of strings, default none. Scope: both, but
each verb is SUT scoped (see Needs table). Lint rejects unknown
verbs. Runtime keeps a skip branch for verbs the linter cannot know.
Code wins: unknown fails lint today; it would skip if it ever
reached runtime. Omit needs when the file uses only the base
abilities.

  needs:
    - workload.submit

domain. Required on weld, forbidden on concord. Int 0..101. Scope:
weld-only. Picks the DDS domain id passed to every weld spawn. A
concord file with domain fails lint; a weld file without it fails
lint.

  domain: 64

topic. Optional on weld, forbidden on concord. Scope: weld-only.
Shape: leading slash, then alnum, slash, or underscore, max 127
chars. Default: derived per run as a chatter path with run id, pid,
seconds, and sequence. Explicit topics pin one channel; derived
topics keep parallel runs apart. If parallel runs hear each other,
you pinned one topic where the default would have split them.

  topic: /chatter_demo

spawn. Optional mapping, default plain spawn with no args. Keys:
variant, node, offset. variant is plain, anchor, or skew. node is an
int 0..nodes-1. offset is a non-empty string, max 63 chars. Scope:
variant, node, and offset are concord-only when non-plain. On weld,
variant must stay plain and node plus offset must be absent. skew
needs node plus offset; anchor needs node. Plain ignores node and
offset at runtime. skew sets the clock offset on one node; anchor
starts one node with anchor and points configs at it. If skew lacks
node or offset, lint fails.

  spawn:
    variant: plain

  spawn:
    variant: anchor
    node: 0

  spawn:
    variant: skew
    node: 1
    offset: "100ms"

ros_peers. Optional list, default none. Scope: weld-only. Lint never
requires it; it is omittable in every file. Semantics: entries start
ROS peers beside weld nodes. Each entry is a mapping with node (int
0..nodes-1) plus distro (humble, jazzy, or rolling). List must be
non-empty when present, max 100 entries. Add an entry only when an
observed check must also see the text in that node's ROS capture
(see observed). Extra peers slow the run for no verdict gain.

  ros_peers:
    - node: 0
      distro: jazzy

bins. Optional mapping, default none. Scope: both. Maps a node
index to a binary path for mixed-version runs. Each key is a node
index 0..nodes-1; each value is a non-empty path string, max 191
chars. The map must be non-empty when present. Origin need:
spawn.bins on either SUT. Missing binaries skip the run like a
missing default binary. Concord boots the default binary, then
preserve-restarts each listed node once into its binary at
startup (the driver has no per-node spawn verb, so bins is a
spawn plus per-node restarts). Weld passes each per-node bin
straight to its per-node spawn.

  bins:
    1: ./bin/concord-v2

disk_cap_mb. Optional int, default 0. Scope:
concord-only. Boot state cap in MB, 0..65536;
0 means uncapped. A nonzero cap boots through
concord_spawn_ex, which mounts a private tmpfs
of that size over the node state dir, on the
plain and bins paths alike. Restarts never
carry a cap. On weld, or above 65536, lint
fails. No check/ shape exists; the driver verb
is concord_spawn_ex in driver/concord.h.

  disk_cap_mb: 512

timeout_s. Required. Int 1..3600. Scope: both. File backstop: any
phase still running past this fails with file timeout.

  timeout_s: 45

phases. Required. Non-empty list of phase maps. Scope: both.
Duplicate phase names fail lint.

  phases:
    - name: mesh
      until:
        peers: 2
        on: all
      timeout_s: 45

Phase keys

Each phase does steps on entry, then waits for one condition or
holds one invariant until its timeout. Keep phases small and
ordered: prove mesh first, then fault, then reunite. Every reunion
verdict assumes a meshed start, so a file that faults before proving
mesh proves nothing about recovery.

Phase map allows exactly 7 keys: name, do, until, sustain, hold_s,
timeout_s, multicast. Unknown keys fail lint.

name. Required. Non-empty string, max 63 chars. Scope: both. Must be
unique per file.

  name: mesh

do. Optional. List of step maps, default empty. Scope: both. Steps
run in file order on phase entry, before the poll. Each step map
holds exactly one verb key.

  do:
    - drop: all

until versus sustain. Exactly one of the two must be present, never
both, never neither. Scope: both. until waits for its one condition,
latches, waits out hold_s, then advances; flap clears the latch.
sustain must hold for the whole timeout_s; any violation fails at
once. Pick until to prove something appears; pick sustain to prove
something never leaks. Mixing them fails lint.

  until:
    peers: 2
    on: all

  sustain:
    absent:
      text: weld-b
      on: [0]

hold_s. Optional. Int 0..3600, default 0. Scope: both. Needs until;
hold_s with sustain fails lint. Must not exceed the phase timeout_s.

  hold_s: 8

timeout_s. Required. Int 1..3600. Scope: both. Phase backstop.

  timeout_s: 45

multicast. Optional. String on or off, default on. Scope: both.
That phase pumps through tun_pump_fd_nomcast, so IPv4 multicast
(224.0.0.0/4) goes blind while unicast gossip, sync, and Noise
keep flowing: anchor discovery alone must carry the phase. Use it
with a skew or anchor spawn to prove discovery without multicast.
Origin need: net.nomcast is concord-only; a weld file that
declares it fails lint, but the phase key itself works on both.

  multicast: off

One condition per phase. A condition map holds exactly one of peers,
marker, present, absent, observed, assigned, mesh_count, agree,
assigned_on, epoch, image. Chain
phases for AND. Named workloads allow several submits per file;
a bare second submit fails (see Second-submit rule in Limits).
Mesh before fault: authoring rule, not lint.
Prove mesh in an early phase (peers or mesh_count), then fault in
later phases.

Step verbs (do entries)

Steps change the world on phase entry: shape links, heal them, slow
them, kill or restart nodes, move nodes, submit, update, or stop
work, publish or replay chatter, or pressure disks. Each entry holds
exactly one verb. Unknown verbs fail lint. Node
indices are ints 0..nodes-1, quoted or bare. SUT scope is enforced
per verb below: a concord-only verb on a weld file fails lint, and
reverse.

drop. Scope: both. Two shapes. Bare shape: the scalar all or a
non-empty list of node indices. It marks legacy full drops in the
poll pump. Use it to open a split; follow with heal to close it.

  - drop: all

  - drop: [1]

Fault shape: a mapping with nodes plus any fault keys. nodes is all
or a non-empty index list. Any fault key present selects the QoS
path (even dir alone). Keys: loss 0..1 float, delay_ms 0..3600000,
jitter_ms 0..3600000, kbps 0..1000000 (0 means uncapped), dup_pct
0..100, dir out, in, or both (default out). Out shapes the listed
fds; in shapes every other fd (sender-side approximation, also
shaping third-party traffic); both shapes all fds. QoS mirrors
tun/tun.h: loss is deterministic from the seed, delay is never
slept by the pump, kbps is a token bucket, dup resends a copy.
Origin need: net.qos is concord-only; a weld file that declares
it fails lint, but the step itself works on both. Ranges outside
fail lint loudly.

  - drop:
      nodes: all
      loss: 0.2
      delay_ms: 50
      jitter_ms: 5
      dir: out

heal. Scope: both. Bare shape only: the scalar all or a non-empty
list of node indices. Fault keys never appear on heal; a mapping
fails lint. Heal clears all pump faults at once: every QoS slot
plus every directed edge, globally, plus the legacy drop marks in
its scope (listed nodes, or all for heal all). A listed heal still
clears QoS everywhere; only the legacy marks stay scoped.

  - heal: all

  - heal: [1]

kill. Scope: both. Mapping with node plus signal. signal is KILL or
TERM, mapped to SIGKILL or SIGTERM. Weld kills through the weld
path; concord kills through the concord path. KILL is abrupt; TERM
lets the node trap and exit.

  - kill:
      node: 0
      signal: KILL

restart. Scope: both for fresh and preserve; concord-only for
rekey_rotate, key_wipe, and wipe_state. Mapping with node plus
durability. Durability names: fresh, preserve, rekey_rotate,
key_wipe, wipe_state. Weld with a durability past preserve fails
lint. Runtime maps weld fresh and preserve to its two restart modes,
and concord names to its five modes. preserve keeps state; fresh
wipes it; the three key modes rotate or drop keys. Picking
wipe_state on weld fails lint.

  - restart:
      node: 0
      durability: fresh

  - restart:
      node: 0
      durability: preserve

  - restart:
      node: 1
      durability: rekey_rotate

  - restart:
      node: 1
      durability: key_wipe

  - restart:
      node: 1
      durability: wipe_state

move. Scope: both. Mapping with node plus x_m. x_m holds meters;
grammar is decimals: digits, dot, e or E science; no hex, no leading
space; range -1e6..1e6. Runtime scales meters to nm. Out-of-range or
hex fails lint.

  - move:
      node: 2
      x_m: 7.0

submit. Scope: concord-only. Mapping with node, image, and
optional flags, as, count, id, rate. image is a shell-safe docker
ref: alnum plus _ . : / @ + -, max 127 chars. flags is optional;
charset is alnum plus _ = . , space / - %, max 127 chars, and may
hold %host tokens (see Memory %host flags). as names the workload:
charset [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+, max 63 chars, default submitted. count is
optional, 1..100, and needs as: counted submits mint members
BASE-0..BASE-N-1, and the base name must leave room for the
suffix (base longer than 60 chars fails lint). rate is optional,
1..1000000 msgs per sec; it paces counted submits with sleeps
between issues and is accepted but unpaced on single submits.
id is optional, non-empty, max 63 chars; the concord submit path
cannot set IDs (the CLI mints UUIDs), so a submit with id ends
the run as SUITE_SKIP with reason fixed ids unsupported, never
faked. The second-submit rule is name based, not single flight
(see Limits). Submitting on weld fails lint.

  - submit:
      node: 0
      image: probe
      flags: "a=b"

Named workload submit:

  - submit:
      node: 0
      image: probe
      as: w0

Counted paced submit (see bulk.yaml):

  - submit:
      node: 0
      image: probe
      as: bulk
      count: 3
      rate: 2

stop. Scope: concord-only. Mapping with optional workload, default
submitted; charset [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+. Stops every set member on its
submit node and wants the Stopped line. A base name stops the
whole counted set. Lint fails stop with no earlier submit.

  - stop:
      workload: submitted

pub. Scope: weld-only. Mapping with from, text, and optional
topic, rate. from is a node index. text is a non-empty string, max
255 chars. topic is optional with topic shape, max 127 chars.
Default topic is the file topic (explicit or derived). rate is
optional, 1..1000000; it is parsed but currently unpaced, advisory
only. Pub on concord fails lint.

  - pub:
      from: 0
      text: weld-probe

  - pub:
      from: 1
      text: hello
      topic: /chatter_demo

benchmark. Scope: weld or concord by key family. Meaning lives in
docs/benchmark; shape lives here. Weld family needs from, on, plus
text or payload_bytes; plus rate or ramp; plus count and/or
duration_s; optional topic, warmup, echo. Concord family needs
node, image, as; plus rate or ramp; plus count and/or duration_s;
optional flags, warmup. Bounds: rate 1..1000000; weld count
1..1000, concord 1..100 (each issue mints a workload); duration_s
1..3600; without ramp, at least one of count or duration_s, and
both means first bound wins; warmup default 0, below count when
count is present; rate times duration_s stays under the family
cap; payload_bytes 0..65536, 0 means text. ramp block takes
start_rate, step_rate, step_duration_s, max_rate, max_steps 1..10,
max_duration_s, plus loss_pct 0..100 and/or p99_ms 0..3600000, and
forbids top rate, count, duration_s, warmup. from: ros:N needs
ros.pub; echo: needs relay.echo. Bad pairs fail naming the
missing verb. Loss plus overrun never fail the step.

  - benchmark:
      from: 0
      text: bench-load
      on: 1
      rate: 5
      count: 3
      warmup: 1

ros_pub. Scope: weld-only. Fires ROS pubs outside benchmarks; no
stats emit. Mapping with from, topic, plus text or payload_bytes,
plus rate, plus count and/or duration_s. from names weld or ros
(both mean ROS publish on that node); concord fails, no pub verb.
Needs ros.pub. Bounds match the weld benchmark family
(payload_bytes 0..65536; rate 1..1000000; count 1..1000;
duration_s 1..3600; rate times duration_s under 1000). Meaning:
docs/benchmark.

  - ros_pub:
      from: 0
      topic: /chatter
      text: ros-fire
      rate: 2
      count: 3

slow. Scope: both. Mapping with nodes plus secs. nodes is all or
a non-empty index list. secs is 1..3600: deferred drain through
TunQos slow_secs. Origin need: net.slow is concord-only; a weld
file that declares it fails lint, but the step itself works on
both.

  - slow:
      nodes: [1]
      secs: 5

update. Scope: concord-only. Mapping with image plus optional
workload (default submitted). image uses image shape, max 127
chars. The run shells workload update --image <img> <wid> on the
submit node for every set member, as in check/concord_update.c.
A base name updates the whole counted set. Origin need:
workload.update. Update with no earlier submit fails lint.

  - update:
      workload: w0
      image: probe:v2

replay. Scope: weld-only. Mapping with file plus optional topic
and from. file is a non-empty path string, max 191 chars. topic
is optional with topic shape; default is the run topic. from is
an optional node index, default 0. It publishes a text log, one
payload per line, in order from that node. Empty lines are
skipped; lone CR ends are stripped too. Lines cap at 1024 chars;
longer lines fail the run loudly. Lint checks shape only; files
may appear at run time, so an unreadable file fails the run
loudly, never the lint. There is no rate key on replay.
Concord rejects replay at lint: no equivalent verb exists.
Origin need: weld.replay.

  - replay:
      file: ./msgs.txt
      from: 0

diskfill. Scope: concord-only. Mapping with node plus mb. mb is
0..65536; 0 removes the filler. It calls concord_disk_fill
exactly: fallocates node filler.bin to mb MB. ENOSPC is
best-effort success. Origin need: disk.fill.

  - diskfill:
      node: 0
      mb: 16

diskreadonly. Scope: concord-only. Mapping with node plus state.
state is on or off. It calls concord_disk_readonly exactly:
chmod a-w (on) or u+w (off) over the node state tree, bounded by
10 s. This is an approximation, not block-device RO: root
bypasses it, and off fully reverses it. Origin need:
disk.readonly.

  - diskreadonly:
      node: 0
      state: on

journalcorrupt. Scope: concord-only. Mapping with node. It calls
concord_journal_corrupt exactly: truncates the node journal.jsonl
then writes CORRUPT plus newline, 8 bytes total, so readers see
invalid JSON on next scan. Origin need: journal.corrupt.

  - journalcorrupt:
      node: 0

Conditions (until or sustain)

Conditions read the system: log grep, workload lists, journal
queries, or liveness counts. Each phase holds exactly one.
On-selectors pick which nodes must show it. SUT scope differs per
condition below: using a concord-only check on a weld file fails
lint. Chain phases when you need AND.

peers. Scope: concord-only. Oracle: full mesh verdict marker. Greps
each target node log for peers count with and without space. Value
is a count 1..100 and at most nodes. on is required, all or list,
never any.

  until:
    peers: 2
    on: all

marker. Scope: both. Oracle: generic grep. Raw log substring in per-
node logs; weld logs on weld, node logs on concord. Value is a non-
empty string, max 255 chars. on is required, any, all, or list. any
passes when any node shows the string. Use it for free substrings;
use text or observed for shaped checks.

  until:
    marker: peer.seen
    on: any

present. Scope splits by form. Oracle: workload form reads concord
workload lists; text form greps weld logs. Mapping with one of
workload or text, never both, plus required on as all or list,
never any. No key defaults to the submitted workload ref. workload
form is concord-only: optional workload name, default submitted. A
counted base name means the whole set: every member on every
on-node. text form is weld-only: non-empty string, max 255 chars.
Workload present polls lists every 2 s and wants the short id on
every target. Text present wants the string in every target weld
log.

  until:
    present:
      workload: submitted
      on: all

  until:
    present:
      text: weld-a
      on: [0]

absent. Same keys and scope as present, negated, with the same
optional workload default. A counted base name means the whole set.
Value map is workload or text plus on all or list. Passes when no
target shows it. Used with sustain to prove a split holds: nothing
sent during the cut leaks across.

  sustain:
    absent:
      workload: submitted
      on: [1]

  sustain:
    absent:
      text: weld-b
      on: [0]

observed. Scope: weld-only. Oracle: weld log plus ROS log where a
peer runs. Mapping with on plus text. on is one scalar node index; a
lone single item list counts as scalar. text is a non-empty string,
max 255 chars. The weld log must hold the text; where a ROS peer
runs beside that node, the ROS capture must hold it too. With no ROS
peer on the node, the weld log alone decides. Use it for pub round
trips.

  until:
    observed:
      on: 0
      text: weld-a

assigned. Scope: concord-only. Oracle: global journal query.
Mapping with optional workload, default submitted; extra keys fail
lint. No on key. Passes when any journal shows a non-nil
assignment for every set member. A counted base name means the
whole set. Origin need: journal.assigned.

  until:
    assigned:
      workload: submitted

mesh_count. Scope: both. Oracle: CLI liveness, not log grep. On
concord, CLI alive count on node 0; on weld, live children counted
by kill probe without reaping. Value is an exact count 1..100 and at
most nodes. No on key; extra keys fail lint. Passes on exact match.
Use it for liveness; use peers for mesh verdicts.

  until:
    mesh_count: 2

agree. Scope: concord-only. Oracle: journal AssignedNodeID
unanimity. Mapping with optional workload, default submitted; no
on key, extra keys fail lint. Holds when all nodes report the same
non-nil AssignedNodeID for every set member. A counted base name
means the whole set. Origin need: journal.agree. Journal reads
follow the check/ grep shape (AssignedNodeID tail-1); unreadable
journals read false, never error. See agree.yaml.

  until:
    agree:
      workload: w0

assigned_on. Scope: concord-only. Oracle: per-node journal
AssignedNodeID. Mapping with optional workload (default submitted)
plus required node 0..nodes-1. No on key. Holds when that node
reports non-nil for every set member. Origin need:
journal.assigned_on. Unreadable journals read false, never error.
See agree.yaml.

  until:
    assigned_on:
      workload: w0
      node: 1

epoch. Scope: concord-only. Oracle: per-node AssignmentEpoch.
Mapping with optional workload (default submitted) plus required
node 0..nodes-1 plus optional eq or gte. eq and gte are ints 0 or
more, never both. With no compare it holds when any epoch reads
back; eq holds on exact match, gte on at least. Every set member
must satisfy it. Origin need: journal.epoch. Reads follow the
check/ grep shape (AssignmentEpoch tail-1); unreadable journals
read false, never error. See agree.yaml.

  until:
    epoch:
      workload: w0
      node: 0
      gte: 1

image. Scope: concord-only. Oracle: per-node journal Image.
Mapping with optional workload (default submitted) plus required
value plus required on as all or list, never any. value uses image
shape, max 127 chars. Holds when every on-node journal reports
that image for every set member. Origin need: journal.image.
Reads follow the check/ grep shape (Image tail-1); unreadable
journals read false, never error. See update.yaml.

  until:
    image:
      workload: w0
      value: probe:v2
      on: all

Two distinctions that past readers confused:

peers versus mesh_count. peers is concord-only log grep for the mesh
verdict marker on listed nodes. mesh_count is any-SUT CLI liveness
with no on: concord node list count, or weld live child count. Mesh
verdicts want peers; liveness wants mesh_count. Do not swap them: a
live but unmeshed cluster passes mesh_count and fails peers.

text versus marker versus observed. Text form of present or absent
is weld-only content grep on listed nodes. marker is generic
substring grep on either SUT with any, all, or list. observed is
weld-only pub or sub round trip on one scalar node: weld log plus,
where a ROS peer runs, the ROS log. Round trips want observed;
content holds want text; free substrings want marker.

Needs table

Needs lists driver abilities the file requires. Lint checks each
verb against the file SUT; unknown verbs fail lint. Runtime would
skip unknown verbs it cannot know. Declare only what the file uses:
extra needs narrow where the file can run for no reason, missing
needs hide as skips. Known verbs:

concord (21 verbs):

  workload.submit
  workload.list
  workload.stop
  workload.update
  node.list
  journal.assigned
  journal.agree
  journal.assigned_on
  journal.epoch
  journal.image
  journal.corrupt
  journal.has
  disk.fill
  disk.readonly
  net.qos
  net.slow
  net.nomcast
  spawn.plain
  spawn.anchor
  spawn.skew
  spawn.bins

weld (12 verbs):

  weld.pub
  weld.echo
  weld.replay
  ros.peer
  ros.echo
  ros.pub
  relay.echo
  restart.fresh
  restart.preserve
  spawn.bins
  weld.log
  ros.log

Note: restart.fresh and restart.preserve are weld-only verbs now.
Concord restart steps need no verb; declare the spawn or disk or
journal verb the phase actually queries.

Example:

  needs:
    - workload.submit
    - spawn.plain

Worked example: weld_roundtrip

A four-phase weld file that proves delivery, proves the reverse
path, proves a split holds, then proves reunion restores delivery.
Read it when authoring your first file: it shows probe, reverse,
split with sustain, and reunite in order. File is
suite/examples/weld_roundtrip.yaml (nodes 2, domain 64, one ROS peer
on node 0 with distro jazzy, file timeout 150 s). Topic is derived
per run because no topic key is set.

probe. Pub weld-probe from node 0, wait until node 1 shows observed.
Proves basic delivery before faults. Gate: observed on 1. Fails on
pub error or 20 s without the text in the node 1 weld log (and ROS
log only where a peer runs; node 1 has none).

up. Pub weld-a from node 1, wait until node 0 shows observed. Proves
the reverse path. Gate: observed on 0, which also checks the ROS
capture because node 0 runs a peer. Fails on pub error or 40 s
without dual log match.

split. Drop node 1, pub weld-b from node 1, sustain absent of weld-b
on node 0 for 10 s. Proves the cut holds: nothing sent during the
split leaks across. Gate: sustain absent text on 0. Fails at once if
weld-b appears on node 0, or on heal or pub error.

reunite. Heal node 1, pub weld-c from node 1, wait until node 0
shows observed within 60 s. Proves reunion restores delivery. Fails
on pub error or 60 s without the dual log match.

Limits

Small fixed rules keep files decidable and runs bounded. Break one
and lint fails before anything runs, except the submit rule which
also fails at runtime as a backstop. Read these before adding a
second workload or a second check to a phase.

Single condition. One per phase map; two keys fail lint. Chain
phases for AND.

Second-submit rule. Names open flights, not single flight. A bare
submit (no as:) while any workload is open fails at lint and at run
time; a second submit must carry a fresh as: name. Duplicate names
fail lint and runtime. Counted bases register too: stops, updates,
and workload conditions may name a base (whole set) or a member
(BASE-N). Stop, update, or a workload condition with no earlier
submit fails lint. At most 128 workload entries per file; more
fails lint and runtime. Counts outside 1..100 fail lint loudly.

Step verbs. drop, heal, kill, restart, move, submit, stop, pub,
benchmark, ros_pub, slow, update, replay, diskfill, diskreadonly,
journalcorrupt.
Unknown step keys fail lint. Fault keys never appear on heal.
replay is weld-only; diskfill, diskreadonly, and journalcorrupt
are concord-only; update is concord-only; slow and the drop fault
shape work on both. Conditions are the 11 listed above; unknown
condition keys fail lint.

No text bypass: pub topic defaults to the file topic; explicit
per-pub topics must still pass topic shape. Replay has no rate
key; its topic defaults to the run topic the same way.

Timeout clamps. File and phase timeout_s are 1..3600; hold_s is
0..3600. hold_s needs until and must not exceed its phase timeout_s.

Duplicate phases rejected. Repeat names fail lint.

Memory %host flags. flags stay free form, and --memory 60%host
passes lint: lint only checks the charset (alnum plus _ = . ,
space / - %). A token <int>%host resolves in the driver to
MemAvailable times int over 100 in MB via /proc/meminfo, as in
check/concord_preempt.c. <int> caps at 10000; larger fails as
malformed. After %host must come end, space, or comma, else
malformed. A bare or bad percent fails loudly in the driver and
never reaches the shell.

  - submit:
      node: 0
      image: probe
      flags: "--memory 60%host"

Skew usage. variant skew with node plus offset lints, and the run
maps it to the concord skew spawn. offset is a non-empty string,
max 63 chars; the skew.yaml example uses "300". anchor needs node
and points configs at it; plain ignores node and offset. Weld must
stay plain with no node or offset keys. See
suite/examples/skew.yaml.

  spawn:
    variant: skew
    node: 1
    offset: "300"

Rate and latency granularity. rate is advisory pacing, not a
verdict. It sleeps 1e9/rate ns between issues of counted submits;
single submits accept it and issue once. Pub rate is parsed but
currently unpaced. Time to present is coarse: the run polls
workload lists about once per 2 s, so present timing rounds up to
that rhythm.

Progress fractions. The executor prints sub-phase lines shaped as
suite: frac <phase> <num> <den> with plain non-negative ints, num
below or equal den, den above 0. Bad shapes stay silent; progress
never fails the run. Sources are real events only: after each do
step (done steps of total steps), on until node-set transitions at
the 2 s query rhythm (nodes now meeting of nodes in scope), and
about once per second on hold or sustain dwells (elapsed of hold_s
or timeout_s). Global shapes with no node set (assigned, and
unresolved workload reads) print nothing. Consumers ignore
malformed frac lines, never fail.

Timeline events. After every phase begin and done, every applied
step, every first marker observation in a phase, every condition
outcome, every metric, and every verdict, the run prints one
flushed line:

suite: ev {"t":MS,"k":K,"ph":I,"msg":TEXT,"d1":A,"d2":B}

MS is ms since file start (post-lint, pre-setup anchor on the
monotonic clock). K is one of phase, step, marker, metric,
verdict, or cond. I is the phase index. TEXT is JSON-escaped at
the single print site, so malformed output is impossible by
construction. A and B are ints. Conventions: phase msg is begin
NAME (A 0) then done NAME (A elapsed ms); step msg names the verb
(A step index); marker msg is the marker text (A nodes matched);
cond msg is the cond text (A 1 met, 0 unmet); metric msg is
key=value text (A repeats the value when integral); verdict msg is
pass NAME, fail WHY, or skip WHY (A 0, 1, 2). Lint emits no
events. Older suite: prints stay byte-identical.

Metric keys. phase.<i>.ms reports phase durations; maxrss.mb
reports peak RSS via getrusage in MB. Both emit at each phase end.
present.<name>.ms reports per-workload time to present (first
sight minus submit); it emits at phase end when first seen.
Same-ms reads 0 and is skipped, like bench sub-ms, so its
absence reads as no data, not as zero latency.

Benchmarks. docs/benchmark is canonical for what benchmarks
mean (wire format, clocks, matching, endpoints, patterns, sizes,
needs, metric keys). This file keeps key shapes plus lint bounds
(see benchmark and ros_pub steps above); meaning lives there,
shape lives here. Report views consume these suffix shapes: .lat
for per-message latency, .hist.<b> for bucket counts, .rate_ach
for achieved rate, plus .ms, .pkts, .bytes, .ok, .path for units
and links. Unknown suffixes keep fallback graphs and checks.

